/**
 * Lebanon districts -- geography-derived administrative map data
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------
 * The Lebanon national outline and its 8 governorate boundaries below
 * are REAL geographic data (projected from the `@amcharts/amcharts5-geodata`
 * package's lebanonHigh dataset, itself GIS-sourced), so the overall shape
 * of the country and each governorate is accurate, not hand-drawn.
 *
 * No public source of PRECISE district (caza)-level boundary data for
 * Lebanon's 26 districts was reachable from this environment (see the
 * project notes) or available in any npm/pypi package. So each governorate
 * with more than one district is split into its districts using a geographic
 * Voronoi partition seeded at that district's real town coordinate (its seat --
 * e.g. Byblos town for the Jbeil district), clipped exactly to that governorate's
 * true outline, and then the internal cut between each pair of neighboring
 * districts is run through a midpoint-displacement fractal (the same technique
 * used to generate natural-looking coastlines/terrain) so it reads as an organic
 * border instead of an obviously artificial straight geometric line -- computed
 * identically from both sides so adjacent districts still tile exactly, with no
 * gaps or overlaps. That means: the outer edge of every district shape is exact
 * GIS coastline/border data, and the internal cuts within a governorate are a
 * reasonable, organic-looking approximation of the real caza boundaries, not
 * traced from an official cadastral source. Districts whose governorate has
 * only one district (Beirut, Akkar) use that governorate's real outline
 * directly, with no approximation at all.
 *
 * Do NOT use this data for precise legal, cadastral, or navigational reference.
 *
 * Coordinate space: viewBox is `LEBANON_MAP_VIEWBOX` below (0 0 1050 1347),
 * an equirectangular projection (longitude scaled by cos(latitude)) so the
 * real aspect ratio of the country is preserved -- not stretched.
 */
